<h2>The anatomy of the apprehension</h2>
<p>Anticipatory bail — Section 438 of the CrPC, now Section 482 of the BNSS — is built on a factual premise: a reasonable belief that arrest is coming. In a police case that premise writes itself, because the investigation carries arrest powers. In a complaint case, the premise usually fails at the threshold. The Magistrate who takes cognizance of a private complaint issues <em>process</em>, and for most offences that process is a summons: an instruction to appear, not an authorisation to seize. Until the process escalates, there is often no one with both the power and the intention to arrest the accused — and an anticipatory bail application filed at the summons stage asks the court to protect against a danger that does not yet exist.</p>

<h2>The graded ladder in Antil</h2>
<p>The Supreme Court\'s decision in <em>Satender Kumar Antil v. CBI</em> (2022) organised this intuition into a working scheme. Its Category A — offences punishable with imprisonment of seven years or less — expressly covers complaint cases alongside police cases, and prescribes a ladder.</p>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Step 1 — Summons.</strong> Ordinary summons issue first, and may be permitted to be answered through counsel.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Step 2 — Bailable warrant.</strong> Only if the accused fails to appear despite service.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Step 3 — Non-bailable warrant.</strong> Only if the bailable warrant is ignored. At each step, reasons matter: under the provision governing issuance of process (Section 204 CrPC), a warrant in place of a summons is the exception, not the default.</div>
</div>
<p>The same judgment adds the piece that removes most of the terror from the appearance: bail applications in these cases may be decided without taking the accused into physical custody, and interim bail may bridge the gap while the application is considered. The accused who appears in answer to a summons is not walking into a trap; he is walking into a procedure designed to decide his bail on paper.</p>

<h2>Where the genuine exceptions live</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>NBW already issued.</strong> Once a non-bailable warrant exists, apprehension of arrest is real. Even so, the targeted remedy is usually an application to recall the warrant or convert it into a bailable one, supported by an undertaking to appear — faster and cheaper than a pre-arrest petition.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Complaint referred for investigation.</strong> Where the Magistrate, instead of taking cognizance, sends the complaint to the police and an FIR results, the matter becomes a police case, and the ordinary anticipatory bail analysis revives.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Parallel police case.</strong> A complaint case sometimes shadows an FIR on the same facts. The apprehension then flows from the FIR, and the application should be framed on it, not on the complaint.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Special statutes.</strong> Statutory schemes with their own arrest and bail rigours stand apart; the complaint-case logic of Category A does not dilute them.</div>
</div>

<h2>The cost of the wrong application</h2>
<div class="note">
<p>An anticipatory bail application filed where no arrest is in prospect is not merely redundant — it can hurt. It concedes anxiety without securing protection, invites a judicial observation that the apprehension is speculative, and spends the applicant\'s credibility before the forum he may genuinely need later. The disciplined sequence in a complaint case is: read the process actually issued, appear or seek exemption, and reserve pre-arrest remedies for the day the record shows a real threat to liberty. The law protects the cooperative accused generously; it has little patience for pre-emptive litigation against dangers that the Code itself has graded out of existence.</p>
</div>
